  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 324 PM EDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nOn Monday, surface high pressure will move across the Midwest as \nupper-level ridging will begin to build from the lower Mississippi \nValley into the Ohio Valley. What is left of a sfc cold front should \nlinger over southern KY and into TN, with northeast winds moving in \nacross the region. This cooler flow should hold temperatures down on \nMonday, with highs expected to range from around 80 across southern \nIN and the KY Bluegrass region, to the mid- and possibly upper 80s \nalong the TN border.\n\nDuring the middle of the upcoming week, upper ridging will build \nover the region, with low-level flow veering to the south-southwest. \nThis will bring much warmer air into the region, with well above \nnormal temperatures expected Tuesday through at least Thursday. High \ntemperatures are expected to range from the low-to-mid 90s each day, \nwith lows in the upper 60s and low 70s. With subsidence underneath \nthe upper ridge, rain chances should be suppressed Tuesday and \nWednesday, with hot temperatures being the main weather impact. Peak \nheat index values are currently expected to make it into the upper \n90s and low 100s, resulting in moderate to locally major HeatRisk \ncategories.\n\nForecast confidence begins to decrease Thursday into Friday as the \nupper ridge tries to break down across the southeast US. Medium-\nrange ensemble families diverge on how quickly the ridge erodes, \nwith the GEFS breaking down the ridge and bringing higher moisture \nand precipitation chances into the region faster than the ECMWF. If \nthe ridge is more resilient, then Thursday and Friday may end up \nbeing the hottest days of the week, with elevated heat impacts \ncontinuing. In contrast, a weaker upper ridge would bring a gradual \nreturn of shower/storm chances and slightly milder temperatures. As \nwe head into next weekend, it becomes more likely that the upper \npattern will shift enough to allow a sfc cold front to at least \napproach the Ohio Valley, bringing increased rain chances and milder \ntemperatures.",
